        <li>ITIL 4 Foundation</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div></section>
  <section class="about-ai section-wrap" id="working-with-ai"><h2>Working <br />with AI.</h2><div class="prose"><p>On the client side, I’ve delivered two enterprise AI rollouts: a 12-month pilot that went to a 20,000-user launch, and a second client’s AI search and summarization. In both, my part was the delivery and the evaluation. I tested answer quality with the client, worked with the engineers on guardrails and retrieval settings, and kept everything inside the content licences. The engineers built the models and the pipelines.</p><p>Since October 2026 I’ve also been running SuppClaimCheck, a production AI service that checks supplement claims against open-access literature. I designed it, I direct the Claude Code agents that write the code under rules I set, I review every change, I run the evals and I operate it. I work the same way I did on the client rollouts: answers have to be grounded in sources and evaluated before release, and the constraints are written down and enforced in code. The difference is that here every decision is mine. Day to day I use Claude, Claude Code, Codex and ChatGPT for research, documentation and analysis.</p><a class="text-link" href="/portfolio/#suppclaimcheck">Read about SuppClaimCheck <Arrow /></a></div></section>
  <section class="working-style section-wrap" id="working-practices"><div class="section-heading"><h2>How I work.</h2><p>I use just enough structure for people to get the work done, <br class="desktop-break" /> and I follow through on what we agreed.</p></div><div class="working-rows"><article><h3>I start with how people work today</h3><p>Before I settle on a delivery approach I go through the requirements and constraints and look at the current workflows. On the strained accounts I took over, that meant a technical account review before I promised the client anything.</p></article><article><h3>Every action has an owner</h3><p>I check what the business is asking for against what the software can actually do, and I bring in the people who have to decide. After that I keep one list, open to everyone involved, of who owns each action and what it depends on.</p></article><article><h3>I pass on what I learn</h3><p>In my last job I led a weekly peer knowledge-sharing program for a year and helped develop delivery and review frameworks the team reused. The account-review template from my strained-account work was later picked up by colleagues for their own reviews.</p></article><article><h3>Check it with data before trusting it</h3><p>On one account I went through more than a million user-action records with the client to work out which departments needed basic and which needed advanced training. On client AI rollouts I tested answer quality with the users before each phase. On my own service, every change runs against a labelled eval set before it can go live.</p></article></div></section>
